00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:13.009 align:start position:0%
it is maddening to be reminded to  appreciate what we have for someone to  tell us we shouldn't forget to be  grateful it's in conflict with our times  and their emphasis on constant ambition  and striving  the idea of pausing to take stock of  what has gone well to be content with  many things as they are this feels  strange and dangerous like some kind of  losers cancel or the consolation prize  but in truth there is so much that if we  learned we might be dead by nightfall we  will promptly remember to value the  summer night sky the taste of cold milk  old brick walls deserted railway  stations the presence of our partners  beside us in the stillness of dawn we  are ungrateful partly out of a fear a  fear of accepting what we have as if to  pause to appreciate things as they are  might make a soft complacent without the  willpower to take on new challenges  anxiety feels so much more natural it's  been with us from the start  but ultimately it's not heroic or  sensible always to be dissatisfied  relentlessly to focus on everything  that's gone wrong and to obsess about  our humiliations and rejections there  isn't truth far more courage and skill  in being able to identify a hopeful  perspective in knowing how to train and  keep our eyes on what's being more or  less okay what can best help us in this  oddly is the thought of our own death  how soon and how unexpectedly it may  come and how much we'll miss about life  when it does we are constantly  undermined by our failure to retain the  purity and drama of this helpfully  morbid insight which would strike us  once every day at least and to put it to  powerful use in the ordinary course of  life gratitude is the dividend due to us  one for a few moments we begin to assess  our lives with a truer sense of all our  small but significant advantages and  with a frank humble recognition of all  we have to lose and one day will  you
 

